Case Summary: The Murder of Diane Maxwell Jackson

• The Crime (December 14, 1969): Diane Maxwell Jackson, a 25-year-old single mother and telephone operator, was abducted from a company parking lot in Houston. She was forced into a nearby shack, brutally raped, strangled, and stabbed in the abdomen. A witness named Willie Bell saw a Black man flee the scene and found Diane still alive, but she succumbed to her wounds before the police arrived. Her valuables were stolen, and her red Ford Mustang was missing.
• Initial Investigation: That same night, investigators found Diane’s abandoned car just a mile away with the keys in the ignition. Police collected several partial latent fingerprints from the vehicle. Although they cleared Willie Bell and checked the prints against everyone arrested in the weeks following the murder, no suspects were identified, and the case eventually went cold.
• The Investigation Reopened (1989): Driven by his sister's death, Diane’s brother, David Maxwell, changed his plans to become a lawyer and instead joined the Texas State Highway Patrol and later the Texas Rangers. In 1989, he began reviewing her file and asked the Houston Police Department (PD) to re-examine the original evidence. A local newspaper ran an article asking for public help, and the Houston PD began searching for the original latent prints lifted from the car.
• The Technological Breakthrough (July 2003): Once the prints were located, initial searches against local and state databases yielded no results. However, on July 23, 2003, Texas Department of Public Safety technician Jill Kinkade submitted the prints to the FBI's Integrated Automated Fingerprint Identification System (IAFIS)—a national criminal history database that houses over 70 million records. In less than five hours, the system returned a match to the number one candidate: James Ray Davis.
• The Arrest and Confession: Investigators discovered that Davis had a long criminal record and had been released from prison just nine days before Diane's murder. Houston PD Sergeant James Ramsey tracked Davis down near the Texas-Arkansas border. Because the fingerprints were on the outside of the vehicle, police needed a confession for a solid conviction. When confronted with the forensic evidence and crime scene photographs, Davis confessed.
• Justice and Recognition (November 2003 / 2011): On November 24, 2003—34 years after the homicide—James Ray Davis pled guilty in court and was sentenced to life in prison. In 2011, the FBI honored Detective James Ramsey and Technician Jill Kinkade with the "Latent Hit of the Year" Award, which is given annually for solving major violent crimes using the IAFIS database.
